## Battery Chemistry Comparison

| Type | Voltage (V) | Typical Capacity (mAh) | Cold Performance | Self-Discharge |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Alkaline AA** | 1.5V | 2500 - 2800 | Poor | Low |
| **Lithium AA (L91)** | 1.5V | 3000 - 3500 | Excellent | Very Low |
| **CR123A (Lithium)** | 3.0V | 1500 - 1600 | Good | Very Low |
| **9V Alkaline** | 9.0V | 550 - 600 | Poor | Low |
| **NiMH AA (Rechargeable)** | 1.2V | 2000 - 2500 | Fair | High |

### Why Lithium Wins
*   **Voltage Sag**: Alkaline voltage drops linearly. Lithium holds ~1.5V until 95% depleted.
*   **Cold**: At -20°C (-4°F), Alkaline loses ~50% capacity. Lithium loses ~10%.
*   **Leakage**: Lithium won''t leak and ruin your expensive lock.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:12', '2026-02-12 15:49:12', '2026-02-12 16:16:57');
## The Hidden Drain

### Self-Discharge
Every battery loses charge just sitting on a shelf.
*   **Alkaline**: ~2-3% per year.
*   **NiMH**: ~1-2% *per day* (older types) or 10-20% per year (LSD). Not recommended for locks.
*   **Lithium (Primary)**: <1% per year. Best for vacation homes.

### Cold Weather Impact
Cold slows down the chemical reaction inside a battery, increasing its internal resistance.
*   **Symptom**: Lock moves slowly or fails to fully extend the bolt on cold mornings.
*   **False Low Battery**: The voltage drop under load (when motor runs) triggers the "Low Battery" warning, even if capacity remains.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:15', '2026-02-12 15:49:15', '2026-02-12 16:16:59');
## Memory Limits

| Lock Grade / Type | Typical Capacity | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Residential (Grade 3)** | 20 - 50 codes | Basic chipsets (e.g., Kwikset Halo) |
| **Prosumer (Grade 2)** | 100 - 250 codes | Schlage Encode, Yale Assure |
| **Commercial (Grade 1)** | 500 - 2,000 codes | Alarm Lock Trilogy, Schlage CO |
| **Cloud-Managed** | Unlimited* | *Stored in cloud, synced in batches to lock |

### Key Takeaways
*   **The "Rotational" Limit**: Even if a lock holds 250 codes, scrolling through them on a keypad for management is a nightmare. Use an App or Web dashboard for anything over 10 users.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:19', '2026-02-12 15:49:19', '2026-02-12 16:17:02');
## Why Rotate?

Static codes are a security risk. They get shared, written on post-it notes, or captured by hidden cameras.

### Rotation Schedule
*   **High Security (Server Room)**: Every 30-90 days.
*   **Medium Security (Office)**: Every 6 months or upon employee exit.
*   **Low Security (Home)**: Once a year.

### The "Kill Switch"
Always have a master code or admin app that can instantly revoke a credential if it''s compromised.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:21', '2026-02-12 15:49:21', '2026-02-12 16:17:04');
## Why this matters
Incorrect bore hole sizing is the #1 reason for smart lock installation failure. Most US residential locks follow ANSI/BHMA standards, while European locks follow DIN.

| Measurement | US Standard (ANSI) | EU Standard (DIN) | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Bore Hole** | 2-1/8" (54mm) | PZ Standard | Most common deadbolt size |
| **Backset** | 2-3/8" or 2-3/4" | 60mm or 70mm | Distance from edge to center |
| **Edge Bore** | 1" (25.4mm) | - | For the latch bolt |
| **Door Thickness** | 1-3/8" to 1-3/4" | 35mm - 45mm | Thicker doors need kit |

### Key Takeaways
*   **Measure Twice**: Use the template provided in the box.
*   **Adapter Rings**: If your hole is too small (e.g., 1-1/2" deadbolt), you need a hole saw.
*   **Thickness**: Commercial doors (1-3/4"+) often require a "thick door kit".', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:26', '2026-02-12 15:49:26', '2026-02-12 16:17:05');
## Backup Access Matrix

| Method | Used By | Pros | Cons |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **9V Battery Contacts** | Yale, Samsung, Schlage (Keypad) | Universal, cheap battery | Looks industrial, hard to hold while typing code |
| **USB-C / Micro-USB Port** | Eufy, Lockly, Aqara | Modern, power bank accessible | Need a cable + power bank |
| **Physical Key Override** | August, Level, Schlage Encode | 100% reliable | Can be picked/bumped, key management |
| **Mechanical Code (Button)** | Simplex, Kaba | No power needed | Expensive, commercial look |

### Key Takeaways
*   **Always Carry a Key**: If your lock has a keyway, keep a spare key hidden outside or in your car.
*   **Power Bank**: If your lock has USB-C backup, a small lipstick-sized power bank in your glovebox is a lifesaver.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:28', '2026-02-12 15:49:28', '2026-02-12 16:17:09');
## The Difference Explained

### Fail-Safe (Power to Lock)
*   **Unlocked when power creates**: The lock requires electricity to stay locked.
*   **Power Outage**: Unlocks automatically.
*   **Use Case**: Fire exits, office main entries (life safety).
*   **Type**: Magnetic locks (Maglocks).

### Fail-Secure (Power to Open)
*   **Locked when power creates**: The lock requires electricity to unlock.
*   **Power Outage**: Stays locked.
*   **Use Case**: Server rooms, home front doors (security).
*   **Type**: Electric Strikes, Smart Deadbolts.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:30', '2026-02-12 15:49:30', '2026-02-12 16:17:11');
## Installation Time Reference

| Lock Type | Professional Install (Min) | DIY Install (Min) | Complexity |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Retrofit (August/SwitchBot)** | 10-15 | 15-30 | Low |
| **Deadbolt Replacement** | 20-30 | 45-60 | Medium |
| **Mortise Lock** | 45-60 | 90-120 | High |
| **Fresh Install (Drilling)** | 30-45 | 60-90 | High |
| **Wired Electric Strike** | 60-90 | Not Recommended | Very High |

### Factors Affecting Time
*   **Door Condition**: Misaligned doors can double install time.
*   **Material**: Metal doors require special drill bits and time.
*   **Access**: Getting power to the door (for wired locks) is the biggest variable.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:33', '2026-02-12 15:49:33', '2026-02-12 16:17:15');
## The Essentials List

### Must-Haves
*   **Phillips Head Screwdriver (#2)**: For removing old lock and installing new one. Do *not* use a power drill for final tightening (strips screws).
*   **Tape Measure**: To verify backset and door thickness.
*   **Painter''s Tape**: To hold the latch in place during install.

### For Fresh Installs (Drilling)
*   **Hole Saw Kit (2-1/8" & 1")**: Specifically for doors.
*   **Chisel (1")**: To mortise the latch faceplate flush.
*   **Hammer**: For the chisel.
*   **Utility Knife**: To score paint around the old latch.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:37', '2026-02-12 15:49:37', '2026-02-12 16:17:18');
## Annual Maintenance Estimates (Per 100 Units)

| Cost Item | Frequency | Unit Cost | Annual Total |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Battery Replacement** | 1.5x / Year | $2.00 (4xAA) | $300 |
| **Labor (Battery Swap)** | 1.5x / Year | $15 (15 min) | $2,250 |
| **Firmware Updates** | 1x / Year | $0 (OTA) | $0 |
| **Failed Unit Swap** | 2% Rate | $250 (Hardware + Labor) | $500 |
| **Platform Subscription** | Monthly | $5 / Unit | $6,000 |

### Key Takeaways
*   **Labor is the killer**: Paying someone to drive to a site to change batteries costs 10x more than the batteries.
*   **Subscription**: Cloud fees often exceed hardware costs within 3-4 years.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:40', '2026-02-12 15:49:40', '2026-02-12 16:17:21');
## Expenditure Types

### CapEx (Capital Expenditure)
The upfront cost to buy and install.
*   **Hardware**: Lock, Hub, Gateway.
*   **Installation**: Wiring, Door Prep, Setup.
*   *Budget Strategy*: One-time project budget.

### OpEx (Operational Expenditure)
The ongoing cost to keep it running.
*   **Batteries**: Recurring.
*   **SaaS Fees**: Monthly software limits.
*   **Support**: IT time to reset passwords/troubleshoot.
*   *Budget Strategy*: Annual maintenance budget.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:43', '2026-02-12 15:49:43', '2026-02-12 16:17:23');
## Device Caps by Ecosystem

| Hub / Ecosystem | Max Devices | Realistic Limit | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **SmartThings V3** | ~200 | ~64 direct | Needs repeaters for more |
| **Philips Hue Bridge** | 50 | 40-45 | Performance drops near limit |
| **Eero (Zigbee)** | ~50 | ~30 | Varies by router model |
| **Home Assistant (SkyConnect)** | 32 (Direct) | 100+ (Mesh) | Depends on Zigbee Stick |
| **Aqara M2** | 128 | ~64 | Requires repeaters |

### Key Takeaways
*   **The 32-Device Rule**: Most Zigbee coordinators can only handle 32 direct children. You *must* add routers (plugs) to go beyond this.
*   **Hue Limit**: If you hit 50 lights, buy a second bridge.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:46', '2026-02-12 15:49:46', '2026-02-12 16:17:25');
## The Cost of Hops

A "Hop" is when a message jumps from Device A -> Device B -> Hub.
*   **Zigbee Max Hops**: ~15-30 (depending on profile).
*   **Z-Wave Max Hops**: 4.

### Impact on Latency
Each hop adds delay (~10-50ms).
*   **1 Hop**: Instant response.
*   **4 Hops**: Noticeable lag (human perception threshold ~100ms).
*   **Too Many Hops**: Packet loss and "Lock Not Responding" errors.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 15:49:49', '2026-02-12 15:49:49', '2026-02-12 16:17:27');
