title: Hidden Installation Costs
slug: hidden-installation-costs
description: Budget killers to watch out for when planning a deployment.
category: Resources
tags: [glossary]
calculator_slug: installation-cost-estimator
---

## Common "Gotchas"

### 1. Door Alignment Correction ($50-100/door)
Smart locks require effortless deadbolt throw. If your current lock requires you to "push/pull/lift" the door to lock it, a smart lock will fail (jam) or drain batteries in weeks. Adjusting hinges/strike plates is extra labor.

### 2. Door Thickness Adapter ($15-30 kit)
Standard residential doors are 1-3/8" to 1-3/4". Thick entry doors (2" +) require long-screw kits or spindle extensions often not included in the box.

### 3. Wi-Fi Bridge / Hub ($30-80 unit)
"Bluetooth only" locks are cheap but need a bridge for remote access. Don''t forget this hardware cost.

### 4. Re-keying ($20-30/cylinder)
To use one physical key for all new locks, you must pay for re-keying or buy "Keyed Alike" sets upfront.', 8, 1, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', '2026-02-12 16:03:41', '2026-02-12 16:03:41', '2026-02-12 16:17:14');
## What is RSSI?

**Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI)** is measured in dBm (decibels-milliwatts). It is a negative number: close to 0 is better, consistent with "less signal loss".

| RSSI Range (dBm) | Signal Quality | Reliability |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **-30 to -50** | Excellent | Perfect, near hub |
| **-51 to -65** | Good | Reliable, fast response |
| **-66 to -75** | Fair | Occasional latency |
| **-76 to -90** | Poor | Frequent disconnects |
| **< -90** | Unusable | Likely disconnected |

## Link Quality Indicator (LQI)
While RSSI measures "loudness", LQI measures "clarity". A strong signal (-40 dBm) with high interference (microwave, WiFi) can still have low LQI and poor performance.

